WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.379 --> 00:00:03.546
(upbeat lively music)

00:00:06.171 --> 00:00:08.900
- Welcome to episode
three of the Addy Hour.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.490
For any of you who are new listeners,

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.890
happy to have you joining in

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.480
with this podcast conversation.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.490
Really encourage you also to check out

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.940
our first conversations
from the first two episodes.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.020
I think you'll really
enjoy some of the depth

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.650
that we were able to jump
into in both of those.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.790
But for today's episode,

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.600
really excited to welcome two guests

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.130
and we're gonna be discussing
topics related to stigma,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.030
mental health disparities,
youth, and suicide prevention.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.460
So clearly some heavy topics

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.200
but some important topics that
we need to discuss as well.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.040
And I'm really honored to
welcome today's two guests.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.230
The first is Dr. Alfiee Breland-Noble.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.540
Dr. Alfiee is an
internationally recognized

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.560
mental health expert,
scientist, author, speaker,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.370
and media contributor.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.790
She's also the founder
of the AAKOMA Project

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.840
and the host of the
Couched in Color podcast

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.900
where she focuses on mental
health and BIPOC youth

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.700
and more generally in
marginalized communities

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.840
and in marginalized youth as well.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.690
One of the things I really
admire about Dr. Alfiee

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.850
is her effectiveness and skill

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.190
in translating complex scientific concepts

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:13.630
into everyday language.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.010
I think that's a rare
commodity, unfortunately,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.160
but I'm really thrilled that
she's so skilled in that

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.870
and that she's willing to jump
in to today's conversation.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.210
So thanks for being here.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.533
- Of course, my pleasure.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.260
- Our second guest is Doug Middleton.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.750
Doug, I almost gave you a doctor title.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.730
It just kinda flowed out.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.170
Maybe I'm speaking that
into the future, who knows.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.003
- There you go.

00:01:33.003 --> 00:01:33.910
(laughs)

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:36.886
- I'm a degree away so I
got a little work to do.

00:01:36.886 --> 00:01:38.670
- That works, that works.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.500
Doug is an NFL safety for
the Jacksonville Jaguars,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.470
and he's also a mental health advocate

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.580
who is all about action.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.800
As the founder of the Dream
the Impossible Foundation,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.640
Doug has been bringing
mental health awareness

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.470
to communities, to high
schools, to universities,

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.770
and specifically to the
African American community.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.240
So he's used his platform
to really address things

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.840
like mental health risk factors

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.270
to talk about access to
mental health services,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.740
and he also has a large
emphasis on youth development.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.140
He's partnered with many different groups,

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.050
one of which is the National
Alliance on Mental Illness.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.640
So I'm thrilled to welcome
Dr. Doug to the program today.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.095
It's gonna keep rolling--

00:02:16.095 --> 00:02:16.928
- Appreciate it.

00:02:16.928 --> 00:02:18.100
(laughs)

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.060
- We speak those things into the future.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.181
- Amen.

00:02:21.181 --> 00:02:22.173
- And to be clear, I
mean, some of the work

00:02:22.173 --> 00:02:24.190
that you're doing already is
so impactful, both of you.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:25.870
So I'm just honored that
you're both willing to jump

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.023
into this conversation.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.400
- Appreciate you having us.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.530
- So I thought it would also
be helpful for our listeners,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.890
for any who might not be
familiar with you all,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.310
just to get to know you a little bit

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.970
and to just talk about
where we are as a society

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.200
at this moment.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.190
Clearly, it's been a challenging
few weeks in the country

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:45.990
especially with the tragic
shootings in Atlanta,

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.320
and then of course following up

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.520
with tragic students in Boulder.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.570
And so in a lot of ways,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:52.510
I mean, we're all still
going through, as a society,

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.320
a level of loss and mourning
around those two issues,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.380
and especially whenever
there's a mass shooting

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.970
and all the trauma that
can come from that,

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.170
but all the different emotions that come

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.090
in time with that as well.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.420
So I thought, just to be
honest and have people,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.420
have both of you just share
how you're doing right now

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.540
in the midst of everything
that's going on,

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.700
so, Dr. Alfiee, we can go
ahead and start with you.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.533
- Okay, sure.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.579
So a couple of things.

00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:18.090
One thing I think is a
little odd, I'll start there,

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.850
for me in terms of my response.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.543
And I'm sure I'm not alone, but I think,

00:03:21.543 --> 00:03:25.100
because I am a black woman,
the response that I have had

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.110
to the two different shootings

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.650
really centers around this idea

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.610
of how do we respond to
people who commit these acts

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.180
when these people are of
different racial, ethnic groups.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.210
And so the first thing
I heard, first of all,

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.590
Muslim is not a race, right?

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.640
Islam is a religion.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.690
And to hear people talk about

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.710
the second shooter in
that context, it upset me.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:49.740
Number one.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.170
And number two, I look at
the excuses that people may,

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.750
for the white male who
shot those four people

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.543
including mostly Asian women,

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.610
he was having, like, I
literally watched that.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.050
And, like, it makes me teary

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.580
just sitting here thinking about it,

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:08.717
but I was so, I was
livid when I watched it.

00:04:08.717 --> 00:04:11.010
And I think I even tweeted
and put it on social media.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.630
Like, this is how you describe people?

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.780
He had a bad day.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.000
We all have bad days.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.230
All three of us have had bad days.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.070
It's never occurred to
me to go pick up a gun

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.810
and shoot somebody because
I was having a bad day.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:25.200
So that really hurt me because
I have a close girlfriends

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.560
who are Asian American,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.750
my goddaughter is Asian
American and Filipino

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.120
and they're just people in my life

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.100
who I know are deeply impacted by it.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.360
So I hurt because they hurt.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.320
And then on top of all
of that, being black,

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:42.183
we still going through it.

00:04:42.183 --> 00:04:44.010
It's like every day it's somethin' else

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.410
and we always going through it.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.433
So I think it's all of
those things together.

00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:50.985
It's just like the
perfect storm of awfulness

00:04:50.985 --> 00:04:52.942
on top of the pandemic and
being stuck in the house.

00:04:52.942 --> 00:04:54.177
And so all of those things together

00:04:54.177 --> 00:04:55.830
it's just made it really heavy.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.650
So I've had to work really hard

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:00.850
to keep my own mental health in check

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.790
and just kinda keep myself grounded.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.250
So I believe in meditation.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.080
I've meditated before we
even started recording.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.060
So that's what it's been for me,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.520
just really trying to pay attention.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.330
'Cause we're healers, all three of us,

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.300
the work that we do is in healing.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.400
And sometimes when you're a
healer, you're giving so much.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.880
I think we can forget to take care of,

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.610
we gotta heal ourselves too.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.460
So that's been my thing,
trying to pay attention

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.340
and be attending to that and be mindful

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.890
of how I need to take care of myself.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:25.723
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:25.723 --> 00:05:26.940
I mean, those are also important.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:27.773
All those pieces.

00:05:27.773 --> 00:05:28.606
I mean, like you mentioned too,

00:05:28.606 --> 00:05:30.290
this is not happening in isolation.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.600
Everything we've already
experienced in the last year.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.910
And then obviously for us as black people,

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:37.310
all the things that we
experienced on a daily basis

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:38.670
trying to walk through.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.530
I mean, I think there's a level of empathy

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.040
that we're able to have
in these situations.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:43.520
Not to say that others
can't have that too,

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.440
but just some of those common experiences.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.450
And then to see, like you said,

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.440
how some of that can be dismissed

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.700
and the focus just shifts
away from the victims

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.670
instead of where it needs to be.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.163
So I appreciate your
honesty and sharing that.

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:58.549
What about you, Doug?

00:05:58.549 --> 00:06:01.040
- I think, for me, one of the things

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.800
that I've been focused on the most over,

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.750
I mean, really since COVID,
really start to happen

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:12.220
is really controlling what
I give myself access to.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.640
I think that's something I've
really focused very hard on.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.660
Because, I mean, you can
turn on the news every day

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.010
and see something horrible
going on in your community,

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:22.990
you could see something horrible

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.970
going on the other side of the country.

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.560
And yes, I'm emotionally
affected by the shootings

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.710
that's taken place in
Colorado and Georgia.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.730
But to not mention that,
I mean, African Americans

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:38.460
have been suffering a
lot this year with COVID.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.397
So I definitely don't
agree with any things

00:06:41.397 --> 00:06:42.810
that's going on in this country,

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:44.670
but at the same time I
have to start to control

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.590
some of the things that I see.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:48.993
Because, I mean, it's every
day that there's something.

00:06:48.993 --> 00:06:51.393
That every day that is
something that's going on,

00:06:51.393 --> 00:06:54.210
that somebody is being killed
and somebody is being hurt.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:57.980
And like you said, as healers,

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.660
it's hard to not wanna feel like

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:02.590
we wanna do something to change that.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.043
I even flashed back to when George Floyd,

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:12.980
during that incident and how
I felt when I was on vacation.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.780
I was on vacation with my fiance

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.890
and we like logged on to Twitter

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.580
and just seeing all the videos and stuff

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.170
and like every time you looked
it was just something there

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.330
and it was like we couldn't
even really enjoy our vacation.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.740
We really just left and went home

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.010
because we felt like
we had to do something.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:32.410
And that moment right
there kinda showed me

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.100
how I have to control
the things that I see

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.470
and how much it impacts my
own personal mental health.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.460
So for me, I see those things

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.230
and I try to do the
best that I can to heal

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:44.120
and make a difference.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.433
But at the same time there's
only so much that I could do.

00:07:47.433 --> 00:07:51.896
I can't go out there and
change gun control policies.

00:07:51.896 --> 00:07:54.270
I can't go out there and
change the way people feel

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.672
about other ethnicities.

00:07:56.672 --> 00:07:58.273
So it's tough.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.430
And I think the best thing that I can do

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.780
is focus on the things that
I can change from day to day.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:08.510
I mean, even things as small as,

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.230
one of my tenant's dog died

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.990
and that was the only thing she had

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:13.323
when she moved over here.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:15.410
She had a tough breakup

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.060
and she moved on the other
side of the country after it

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:19.480
and all the things she's had is her dog

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:20.420
for the last 12 years.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.180
And people might say, oh,
that's just something small.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:25.110
Well, I mean, that's
something huge to her,

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:26.070
that's something huge to me.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.750
So it's just so much going on.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.770
You really have to control
what you put into your mind

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.163
and what you give yourself access to.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:36.140
- Yeah, that's so true.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.338
And like you said, it's
that balancing act too.

00:08:38.338 --> 00:08:40.170
'Cause if you're empathetic
and being the healer,

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:41.610
you still wanna be able to reach out.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.970
But if you're tank is completely empty,

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:46.650
then you're not gonna be
any good for anybody else.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.460
And Dr. Alfiee, I know you've
talked about that too a lot

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.020
in terms of the same
thing Doug has mentioned,

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:53.170
how much we consume, like where
do we set those boundaries

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.813
to be informed but not just
be so overwhelmed with it

00:08:55.813 --> 00:08:57.580
that it just drags us down to a place

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:59.040
that we can't heal others.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.370
And then at the same time,
I mean, we're healers

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.520
but we also need to be healed.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.770
So both things are
happening at the same time.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.850
It's not like we're always
in that place of full healing

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:07.810
and going out to help others.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:08.840
We're still, like you said, Doug,

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.250
kind of going through it ourselves too.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.180
So definitely balancing that.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:13.280
- Yeah.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.163
For me, I know like, if I see too much,

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:18.950
sometimes I try to help,

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.770
but if I see too much and
something is going on,

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.250
I just shut down because it's like,

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.120
well, is my impact even
making a difference?

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.160
Am I even like creating change?

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.650
I was like, there's just so much going on

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:28.890
that I'll never be able to help people.

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.220
So that's kinda why I
personally have to filter

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.260
some of the things that I see,

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.060
because it prevents me from shutting down

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:38.490
and kinda just like going into reserve

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:41.107
where I realize that there's
nothing really I can do.

00:09:41.107 --> 00:09:43.170
The worst gonna be the way it is.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.490
- Yeah, but what I like
about what Doug is saying

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.570
is, as a person who's a
mental health advocate,

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.130
the recognition that you do have to,

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.670
I phrase it as curating your news.

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:54.720
So you have to be careful,

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.520
just like Doug said, I
can't say it any better,

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:58.860
about how much you consume,

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:02.640
you also wanna be careful
about what you consume, right?

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.330
So I tell people, I
still haven't to this day

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:07.350
watched the video of George Floyd.

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:09.980
And the closest I came was, I was blessed,

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.840
I had an opportunity to do CNN,

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:13.990
like, literally, I think it was that week

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:14.970
and they showed it.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.820
And I like literally wouldn't look.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.660
I just didn't look at the screen
while they were showing it.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.760
'Cause I don't wanna see that.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.130
'Cause I know me, I'm anxious.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.930
And so that would be
stuck in my brain forever.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:26.100
Once you see it, you can't unsee it.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.340
And then people will say stuff like,

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:29.300
yeah, but you ain't woke if you don't,

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:30.133
you ain't down for that.

00:10:30.133 --> 00:10:33.010
I'm like, nah, man, I'm more
woke if I take care of myself.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:36.060
'Cause if I take care of
myself on the front end,

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.520
I can have a longer opportunity

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.270
and more opportunity to go have an impact.

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:44.460
But if I'm like riddled and
like curled up in a ball

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.370
'cause of how awful it was

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:47.680
and thinking about his little girl,

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.540
and I'm just, like all of that,

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.620
like how many people are
hurt because of that?

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:52.843
And it was so senseless.

00:10:54.173 --> 00:10:55.300
That on a dollar bill or something.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.400
So I have to be careful

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:58.717
about the ways in which I consume it.

00:10:58.717 --> 00:11:00.980
And so I would just add to what Doug said.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:03.810
I tell people, you can't
watch that stuff all the time.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.830
If you wanna be informed,
sometimes you need to just

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:09.920
go to a website and read or pick up,

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.410
anybody who does use the newspaper,

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.370
pick up a piece of paper and read

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.440
'cause then you have a
little bit more control.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.906
But just sitting and watching,
I'm with him, I can't do it.

00:11:19.906 --> 00:11:21.983
- Yeah, exactly right.

00:11:21.983 --> 00:11:23.903
- Yeah, that's important.

00:11:23.903 --> 00:11:26.310
So I mean, all these
topics are so, so critical

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:27.720
and I'm curious how, because both of you

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.770
have so much work that you do with youth.

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.600
So if you could actually
share a little bit

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.420
about your journey into that work

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.080
and then also talk about
some of these principles

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.510
and how those come together,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.090
like how do you pass on
some of this knowledge

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:39.693
to the youth that you're working with?

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:42.685
- Okay.

00:11:42.685 --> 00:11:44.500
Such gentleman, I love it.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:45.333
Just gentleman.

00:11:45.333 --> 00:11:46.440
I just eat it up.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:47.740
I need to get my son in here and like,

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.737
this is how you treat women.

00:11:49.737 --> 00:11:51.560
But I thank you for that, Doug.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.930
So the AAKOMA Project, long story short,

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:58.730
really came out of a
research lab that I had.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:01.500
I founded it in 1999, when I was three.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.333
I'm just kidding.

00:12:02.333 --> 00:12:05.540
And so when I founded the lab,

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.370
we were really all about
trying to understand

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:12.013
how do we encourage
black youth specifically?

00:12:13.278 --> 00:12:14.111
My thing was black kids.

00:12:14.111 --> 00:12:16.160
So AAKOMA started as the acronym

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.180
African American Knowledge Optimized

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.110
for Mindfully Healthy Adolescents.

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:23.830
So my whole thing was just,
what can we do to help

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:27.170
our young black kids build their brains,

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:28.960
build their, not just resilience,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.650
but learn how to take care
of themselves emotionally.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.810
Because back then, that was the '90s,

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.593
nobody was talking about it
like we talk about it now.

00:12:35.593 --> 00:12:37.430
It just was not a thing.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:41.730
And as a black person
with my white colleagues,

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:43.730
God bless them, but my white
colleagues would say things

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:45.730
like, well, how is depression or anxiety

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.030
any different in black kids
than it is in anybody else?

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:48.863
And my thing will be,

00:12:48.863 --> 00:12:51.070
well, we don't have to
unpack that a little bit

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:54.660
because black children specifically
and other kids of color

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:56.860
have to deal with racism
and discrimination.

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:59.590
So for one, that's a huge difference,

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:02.590
not to mention when you
layer on multiple identities

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:05.110
like gender identities
or sexual orientation

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.280
or whether people are
gender conforming or not,

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:09.510
and so that was really where we started.

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:12.400
How do we get kids into care?

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.780
At the time, I said, it's not
my job to fix what's provided

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:16.910
once they get into care,

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.760
once they get in their
bottom in that chair.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.850
Now, it is some of that too,
but we gotta get them in

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:24.470
because they can't benefit
from the treatments

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:26.190
if nobody's going, right?

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.180
So that's where we started.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.420
Over time we evolved.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:32.440
And then in 2019, 20 years later,

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.530
we became a full-fledged 501c3.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.500
So I stopped being a traditional academic.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:38.460
Less people have heard me tell this story,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:40.900
because it was, in my
experience, I'm an NF1,

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:42.260
it was too restrictive.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.950
And so I couldn't do the
kinds of things I wanted to do

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:48.750
like name racial trauma as an
issue that impacts black kids

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.467
and kids of color and study that.

00:13:50.467 --> 00:13:52.420
And so I said, okay, I gotta
do something different.

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.180
So I left traditional academia

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:58.040
after 17, 18 years in
departments of psychiatry

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:59.890
and we turned AAKOMA into a nonprofit.

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:03.050
And now, what AAKOMA is
focused on is three things.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:04.350
We raise consciousness, right?

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:06.130
So we wanna raise people's awareness

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:07.480
about these issues of mental health,

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.180
just like Doug and what he does.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.570
We empower people.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:13.560
'Cause y'all we know, we know we look like

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.950
and we know how many of
us are never going to see

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:16.910
a mental health professional.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.940
We try to change that,
but we know, as of today,

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:21.900
there are many who are never gonna go.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:24.040
And so we want people to
have tools they can use

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:24.873
in the interim.

00:14:24.873 --> 00:14:27.690
So if they never get in
your office, Dr. Addy,

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:29.560
or if they never get
with somebody like Doug

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:32.780
who can get them into,
direct them to care,

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.010
we want 'em to be able to do something

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:34.870
to take care of themselves.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:36.790
And the most important
thing I feel like we do

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:38.630
is we work to change the system.

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.090
We work to shift the system.

00:14:40.090 --> 00:14:42.020
We want the system to be something,

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.170
the mental health system in particular,

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.040
to be a place where black people

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.170
and people of color feel welcome.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:51.062
Where when you walk in,
if you speak Tagalog,

00:14:51.062 --> 00:14:53.440
like grew up in a very
heavily Filipino community

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:54.930
in Virginia Beach, Virginia,

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.860
close to Pharrell Williams and Timberland,

00:14:57.860 --> 00:14:59.500
we all live within like a seven,

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:01.480
eight mile radius of each other.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.940
And so in that community the populations

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:05.680
that were the biggest that
were of color were Filipino,

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.340
not just Asian, it was Filipinos

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.720
because of the Navy and black folks,

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:14.117
and so we wanna make sure
that the system is set up

00:15:14.117 --> 00:15:16.100
such that if I speak Tagalog,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.590
I should be able to find a
provider who speaks Tagalog,

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:21.070
at least enough to help me do some therapy

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:24.470
or who knows enough
about different cultures.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:25.810
You can't know everything there is to know

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:26.643
about every culture.

00:15:26.643 --> 00:15:27.476
That's impossible.

00:15:27.476 --> 00:15:29.080
But you can be open, right?

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:31.560
I can learn how to say,
"Salamat" and thank you,

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:33.340
which is thank you in Tagalog.

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:35.180
Or I can learn how to say, "Mahal kita,"

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:36.730
which is I love you in Tagalog,

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.610
because that little bit of phrasing

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:41.950
is gonna let somebody
know that I see them.

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:43.490
And to me, that's the
most important thing.

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:47.480
We need systems that allow
our people to feel seen,

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:49.340
to feel valued, and to feel heard.

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:51.144
So that's what we're about at AAKOMA.

00:15:51.144 --> 00:15:54.573
And we're all about research
and outreach in those areas.

00:15:56.386 --> 00:15:59.220
- That's so good.
- I love it.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.260
For me, how I got into this space

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.373
and kind of the work that I'm doing,

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:08.300
one of my best friends, my
best friends since age six,

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.220
AJ Morrison, we lived on
the same street together

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:13.380
in Winston-Salem, where I grew up at,

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.420
and went to high school together.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:17.660
And as we went to college,

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:19.560
our paths began to change a little bit

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:22.990
and AJ started to suffer
from severe depression.

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:27.260
During that time, my mom had
already had a lot of history

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:28.250
in the mental health space

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:30.470
and my brother was starting to transition

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:31.920
and getting that space.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:34.900
Right now, he's a behavioral
health crisis counselor.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:38.940
But there were certain
resources that AJ needed

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.890
that we didn't really have access to

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:44.345
or we really didn't know what was,

00:16:44.345 --> 00:16:45.178
what would will work for him.

00:16:45.178 --> 00:16:47.940
And I think that's, just to go
off on a tangent real quick,

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:51.360
I think that's what scares people away

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.960
from going to see people and get help,

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.780
is like they think like
the first counselor

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:58.530
or the therapist that you
see is supposed to work

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:00.300
and sometimes it takes maybe seeing

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.070
four or five different
therapists or counselors

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.033
before you find some effective therapy.

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:07.020
Same as with medicine.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.423
I think that was one of
the issues that he had.

00:17:11.962 --> 00:17:14.960
So just a learning lesson
from his situation for me.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:18.280
He saw a therapist, he
was on a specific medicine

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.870
but that wasn't the
correct medicine for him.

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:24.260
Maybe he had needed to try
two or three more things,

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:27.150
but at the time we weren't really aware.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:30.660
And since that was four years ago

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:32.880
and since that time
I've learned a lot more

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:35.750
about mental health and
about effective therapy

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:37.161
and effective medicine.

00:17:37.161 --> 00:17:42.161
So since that time, a couple months later,

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:46.670
fast forward today, AJ
suffered a death by suicide.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:48.380
And then I spoke at his funeral

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:52.980
and spoke about mental
health in African community.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:56.403
It was all pretty much all
African Americans there.

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:02.610
And I spoke about
depression, anxiety, trauma,

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.360
and the things that AJ was going through

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:11.360
and how you can pair up therapy
and going to see somebody

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:13.970
and there's professionals in this space

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.600
and I've tried to do all that.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:18.850
And immediately after
I got out the pulpit,

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:20.640
the pastor caught up and he said,

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.530
hey, like, that all sounds good,

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.620
but you just need to pray about it

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.804
and bring all your burdens to the Lord

00:18:27.804 --> 00:18:31.650
and He take care all that.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:36.650
And that is what really
made me get into this space.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:39.820
Because right after that,

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:41.920
there was a ton of people that
came up to me and were like,

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:43.640
hey, I'm suffering too.

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:44.930
Like, I need help.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:46.790
Like, what resources are out there?

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:51.660
And it's like, in our community,
the stigma is so strong.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.500
It's even the same way with my grandma.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:55.400
Like, how she was raised

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.950
and how past generations were raised,

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:03.170
as like we look up so much
to our pastors and religion.

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.130
And I'm a big believer.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:06.010
I'm a huge Christian.

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:08.530
But I was always taught, from my mom,

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.508
that it's prayer plus therapy
or prayer or prayer plus.

00:19:11.508 --> 00:19:12.660
It's not just one thing and other.

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:14.930
Like, I wouldn't put
all these professionals

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:16.870
or these experts on this earth

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:19.720
if it was just only something
that you could take to God.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:22.610
So that's how I kinda got
into the mental health space

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:25.500
and then I wanna just make a difference

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:26.950
in the African American community.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:31.950
And then after I had a few
seasoned injuries in the NFL,

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:35.760
I started to experience
my own mental health.

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:39.930
Mental health, I guess,
I don't know, situations

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:44.740
and that's when I really went
to focus on student athletes,

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:47.090
professional athletes and
helping them empower themselves

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:49.773
to careers outside of football,

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.970
being able to feel purposeful
through their transition

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.270
from the game into whatever they do next.

00:19:57.270 --> 00:20:00.120
And that's kinda where we
are now, what I'm doing.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.490
My foundation now is just working

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:04.210
with African American athletes

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:06.380
at primarily white institutions

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:08.940
and just helping them empower themselves

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:10.690
and figure out how to leverage

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:12.900
the environments that they're in

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:15.670
to lead to success after
they're done playing the sport.

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:20.490
So yeah, that's pretty
much the short spiel.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:25.490
And then I don't wanna go too
long into it, but that's it.

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:26.583
- That's so good.

00:20:26.583 --> 00:20:27.447
Definitely appreciate
your passion in that.

00:20:27.447 --> 00:20:29.390
And I mean, one thing,
the thing that kinda cuts

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:30.360
across both of what you're talking about

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.290
is just that theme of being seen.

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:33.990
Like making sure, one,
that people are seeing

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:37.180
in the first place and
that they know what tools

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:39.420
are available at their disposal.

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:41.160
'Cause like you said, the prayer,

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:42.330
it doesn't remove the prayer,

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:44.470
but we also have all these
tools that God's provided,

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:46.120
all the expertise that
we've been able to develop

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:48.090
through neuroscience,
psychology, psychiatry,

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:49.450
therapy, social work.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:50.797
There's no need to ignore all that

00:20:50.797 --> 00:20:52.430
once they're right there from us.

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:54.070
But Dr. Alfiee, your point too about,

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:56.100
I mean, that could be a
whole another conversation

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.220
if we talk about where the stigma started

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:01.630
and why we don't access those services.

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:04.130
But I mean, all those things
are part of the conversation.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:07.060
So I'm deeply appreciative
of both the efforts

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:09.420
that both of you done to really make sure

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:11.050
that folks are seen and that they know

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:13.420
that there are options in that way.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:14.730
Just elaborate on that a little bit.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.800
Like, what have you seen
that's been effective,

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.540
either one of you, in terms of making sure

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:22.400
that people are seen and
helping people kinda transition

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:24.820
to taking advantage of the resources?

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:26.613
- One of the things that I use,

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.900
and with a lot of college
and high school athletes

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:35.080
or professional athletes,
is group therapy.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:39.810
Group therapy has been a
very effective method for us.

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.330
And one of the ways that I
mean about group therapy,

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:45.990
so we will bring maybe three,
four counselors in there

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.410
and then we'll have everybody
sit around in a circle

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:53.780
and we pretty much just share
like some of the things that,

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:55.630
some of the experiences
that these athletes

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:57.250
may be having on campus.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:02.250
And the crazy thing about it
is a lot of these athletes

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.920
are suffering alone,
are suffering in silence

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:08.020
and there's so many
people that's in the room

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:11.010
or their colleagues or other
athletes that can help them

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:12.510
that's suffering the same way.

00:22:13.625 --> 00:22:16.300
And so that's one thing I
really help them notice,

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:19.010
is that the fact of like,
hey, you're suffering,

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:20.010
like, you need to reach out.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.620
Like, if you reach out, chances are,

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:24.530
like the person across from
you or one of your teammates

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:26.100
are probably suffering
from the same thing.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:29.200
So, I mean, it was even things like, hey,

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:31.170
like I'm the only black
student in my class.

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:35.510
Hey, like, I'm six hours away from home.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:39.830
Hey, I'm getting all these looks

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.030
by people with other race on campus.

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:44.503
Like, I don't know how to
even conversate with people

00:22:44.503 --> 00:22:46.000
with other race.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:50.700
And those things, they were all suffered

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:52.310
from similar experiences.

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:55.840
So it was like, once we
found a way to connect them

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:58.323
and really share the experiences together.

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:04.060
A year later, they much
improved and a lot of progress.

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.030
So that's one of the effective ways.

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:09.730
And of course, anytime you
talk about changing the stigma

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:14.300
is people wanna identify
people that others look up to

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:16.210
and that way that you
can break down the stigma

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:21.210
and that way that people don't
see it as something that,

00:23:21.277 --> 00:23:23.990
oh, these people don't do
that, so why would I do it?

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:27.150
So that's been an effective way.

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:31.300
But yeah, I mean, those are
just two points I will say.

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:33.719
- Yeah, that's really good.

00:23:33.719 --> 00:23:36.070
What I like about what Doug is sharing

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:39.170
is definitely what shows up
in the research literature.

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:43.290
And that is normalizing
it by showing people,

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:45.740
reflecting people, giving people mirrors.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.150
So when they bring in multiple counselors

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:52.300
or they bring in multiple
people who are of color, right,

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:54.880
what you're doing is normalizing
for these young people.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:58.050
There are people out there
who look just like you, right,

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:00.810
or look like your cousin or
your people in them, right,

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:01.810
who do this work.

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:06.000
And so those people it's
not always a perfect match,

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.300
but they may be more likely to understand

00:24:08.300 --> 00:24:10.730
what your individual
personalized experiences are.

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:13.100
Because there's
universality to this issue,

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:16.070
with this idea of,
let's just keep it real,

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:18.820
being black at a predominantly
white institution.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:19.653
I went to three, right?

00:24:19.653 --> 00:24:21.070
My undergrad is from Howard.

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:22.640
So that was heaven for four years.

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:26.610
And then I dove out there into
some of these other spaces

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:27.683
and it won't so heaven, right?

00:24:27.683 --> 00:24:29.010
It's kind of the opposite.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:31.410
And so even little day-to-day stuff,

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:33.210
like we talk about microaggressions,

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.720
if you're a athlete and
everybody knows who you are,

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:38.553
that compounds it, right?

00:24:38.553 --> 00:24:40.190
'Cause everybody knows
who the athletes are,

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:41.950
especially if you go to a PWI,

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.640
like one of my degrees is from Wisconsin,

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.160
there was like 12 black people there.

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.506
And of them 12, 10 of 'em was athletes.

00:24:48.506 --> 00:24:49.990
And if you weren't an athlete,

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:51.230
people assumed you were an athlete

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:53.380
because they had no conceptualization

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:54.880
that black people can go to college

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:56.820
doing something other than,
ain't nothing wrong with sports.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:58.670
Like I said, my brother
played college ball,

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.570
but that's not the only way
that black people go to college.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:05.440
So my spouse, whom I met at
Wisconsin, interestingly enough,

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:08.630
was there getting a PhD in math

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.050
and he's kind of a build, a heavy dude

00:25:11.050 --> 00:25:11.883
and the people were like,

00:25:11.883 --> 00:25:12.970
yeah, you play for the football team?

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:15.060
He was like, no, I'm here
getting a PhD in math.

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:16.440
And they would look at them.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:17.500
They were like, right?

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:19.230
They thought they were
getting pranked or something.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:21.280
And so that kind of stuff,

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.110
you can only have that conversation

00:25:23.110 --> 00:25:25.290
with somebody who understands
what that feels like.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.840
So, no, I might not, as a
mental health professional,

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:29.790
understand what it feels like

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:33.090
to be an athlete in that space, right?

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:34.347
But I do understand what it feels like

00:25:34.347 --> 00:25:37.020
if you don't know the black
girl in the class, right?

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:39.130
And any time a conversation
comes up around,

00:25:39.130 --> 00:25:42.486
anything related to race,
everybody like this, right?

00:25:42.486 --> 00:25:43.319
- That's right.

00:25:43.319 --> 00:25:45.380
- Like, I don't have all the
answers for everybody, right?

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:46.420
That's not what I'm here for.

00:25:46.420 --> 00:25:50.530
And so what he speaks to is this idea

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:54.090
of how do you normalize this concept

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.040
of talking to people about
what's going on with you.

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:59.560
Stop internalizing it and holding on to it

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:01.660
and thinking that you have
to fix it all by yourself.

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:03.590
So that, and that's in the literature,

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:06.470
that's what we have to do with
our young people of color.

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:08.260
Other things that I find are helpful

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:09.790
are also echoing what Doug said,

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:12.640
which is people need to see somebody,

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.590
I think young people in particular,

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:17.780
who's prominent, who has fame,

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:19.980
whom they look up to and admire;

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:23.490
I think often that helps because
it helps young people see

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.310
not only are they not alone,

00:26:25.310 --> 00:26:27.620
but your circumstances alone don't dictate

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:28.610
whether or not you're struggling

00:26:28.610 --> 00:26:30.140
with something psychologically, right?

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:33.150
So having money and fame and
fortune and being in the league

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:35.700
that don't protect you
from mental illness, right?

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:36.600
People struggle.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.220
And so I think that's key.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:40.240
I think some of the other
things that we found

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:42.520
from our research with AAKOMA

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:46.540
is it's not just about racial matching.

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:50.090
It is about this idea, and I
know you know this, Dr. Addy,

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:51.370
cultural competence.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:53.950
So if you go see somebody
and you feel like

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:56.250
they're not even trying
to understand who you are,

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:57.280
you gonna vote with your feet.

00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:58.410
You're just gonna leave, right?

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.039
So if you go in, I'm a chocolate girl

00:27:00.039 --> 00:27:01.620
and I started talking about
light skin and dark skin,

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:03.810
they look at me, oh, but I
thought all y'all was black.

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:04.830
True story.

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:07.550
Like, nah, fam, that's
not how it works, right?

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:09.030
And so I'mma need you to like

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:10.910
have a little bit more
knowledge about this stuff.

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:13.123
And so that's important as well.

00:27:13.123 --> 00:27:16.490
I think it's also important that we test

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:18.110
the interventions that we use,

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:20.830
the way that we share,
provide psychotherapy.

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:24.240
So I heard you speak too,
doctor, neuroscience, right?

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:26.110
So we have neuroscience,
we have psychiatry,

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:28.550
we have psychology, we have social work,

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:30.073
you have nurse practitioners,

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.880
making sure that what we provide

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:38.600
is something that has been
evaluated and assessed

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:41.630
as relevant for the
populations we serve, right?

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:43.210
People want to know that like we assume

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:45.220
people don't have that
much knowledge or interest.

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:46.340
Sure, they do.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.130
I wanna know what is this
stuff you're giving to me,

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:49.527
why are you giving it to me,

00:27:49.527 --> 00:27:51.800
and how does it help people
who look like me, right?

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:52.790
So that's important too.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:54.270
And then the final thing that I will say,

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:56.770
just as an example, there are many others,

00:27:56.770 --> 00:27:59.700
is finding the spaces
where people already are.

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:00.690
Again, this is full circle

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:02.010
'cause this goes back
to what Doug was saying

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:02.940
in the beginning.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:05.690
You bring the care to the people.

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:07.990
Don't always ask the
people to go to the care

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:09.590
because it's stigmatized.

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:11.370
So if they're stigmatized,
you should assume

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:13.495
I ain't trying to come
up in there where you at.

00:28:13.495 --> 00:28:14.710
So if you bring it to me,

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:16.157
maybe I'll look at it
a little differently.

00:28:16.157 --> 00:28:18.000
And so one of the things that we do,

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.200
this book in my background
talks about it too,

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:22.730
one approach is we do work with churches.

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:25.010
So we have to talk with
pastors about please,

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:26.260
just what you said, Doug.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:27.520
I've heard that myself.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:29.440
Don't get up in that pulpit
and say stuff like that.

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:31.150
Here's another way you can frame it.

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:32.410
Or just don't say anything about it.

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:35.000
If you don't believe in
it, don't say anything

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:36.127
because people are watching you.

00:28:36.127 --> 00:28:39.647
And if they hear you, as the
pastor, say, we'll do that,

00:28:39.647 --> 00:28:41.320
you just need to pray, right?

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:42.450
Jesus is your doctor.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.900
If they hear you say that,
that's what they're gonna think.

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:47.670
And so that faith-based
mental health promotion

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:50.380
and finding other ways to
go where the people are,

00:28:50.380 --> 00:28:52.340
bringing the group therapy
to the young people

00:28:52.340 --> 00:28:54.610
those are all super
effective ways, I think,

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:56.780
to get us into care.

00:28:56.780 --> 00:29:00.940
- I think just to add on a
couple of points you said,

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:03.130
'cause like I was, definitely,

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:05.150
you talked about the athlete thing.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:08.590
Because I mean, you sound
like, you speak and sound like

00:29:08.590 --> 00:29:10.310
you were sitting right
in the conversations

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:12.750
that I had with student athletes.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:15.580
So that was the biggest thing that I had

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:18.150
when I was in college, the
biggest issue that I had

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:19.190
going to Appalachian State.

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:21.573
I think it's like 4%
African American there.

00:29:21.573 --> 00:29:25.900
It's like no matter if
you got a hoodie on,

00:29:25.900 --> 00:29:28.070
it says you played football
or anything at all,

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:29.360
like people are gonna look at you and say,

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:30.910
hey, like, don't you play football?

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:32.430
I'm like, how do you even know that?

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:35.040
Like, you can't think of my
name, you can't think anything.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:38.370
But you just think because
I'm a black man on this campus

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:40.070
like I have to play some type of sport.

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:43.160
And that just really stood out

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:45.270
because like all the
athletes we were talking to

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:46.850
they feel exactly like that.

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:49.040
And the cultural competence thing,

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:50.120
I think that's a huge point.

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:53.410
Because I did an event for Super Bowl

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.030
with other like NFL athletes

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:56.790
and they were talking about mental health

00:29:56.790 --> 00:29:58.390
and somebody comes in and asks us,

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:00.993
like, how can somebody within,

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:04.240
from a different background
or race or ethnicity

00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:09.330
really, I guess, be a
counselor or a therapist

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.160
to somebody with a different race,

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:15.820
and what are you looking
for when you reach out for,

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:17.450
when you're trying to get help

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:19.190
and somebody that doesn't look like you,

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:22.218
and I think that's a great
point, cultural competence,

00:30:22.218 --> 00:30:24.080
is being able to understand

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:28.130
the issues that the race may have

00:30:28.130 --> 00:30:31.780
that you may not really know
no much about in the beginning.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:33.570
- Yeah, and you can't
be afraid of it, right?

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:35.860
So if the brothers show up,

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:37.310
like one of the things
that always stood out to me

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:39.960
was I remember watching
Brandon Marshall story.

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:41.530
And then when they got to the part

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:43.227
where they show the
person who diagnosed him

00:30:43.227 --> 00:30:44.720
was an older white guy.

00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:46.850
And I thought to myself,
it would have been,

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.130
not there's anything wrong with it,

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:49.360
but it would've been so nice

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:51.920
for him to have been paired with somebody

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:54.920
from his background, right,
to sorta talk him through.

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:57.630
This is how, what you, BPD might show up

00:30:57.630 --> 00:30:58.840
as somebody who looks like you.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:01.260
Now, I'm not saying the
white guy didn't know that.

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:03.940
I can guess, but I'm not saying he did it.

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:05.370
So I just think it's important

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:09.160
that we have a diversity
of people available

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:12.000
to brothers and sisters so
that they have choices, right?

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:13.040
And just like Doug said,

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:15.050
if you got to go to three or four people

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:17.270
to find that person who's
gonna understand you

00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:19.887
and with whom you have a connection,

00:31:19.887 --> 00:31:22.430
we gotta keep encouraging
people to keep looking.

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:23.560
If that first one don't work,

00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:25.410
you shop like you shopping for some shoes.

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:28.010
So you a sneaker head,
you gonna keep looking,

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:30.120
keep looking and shopping
for those providers as well.

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:31.840
So I'm just glad he brought
that point up, though,

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:34.180
about with our athletes.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:35.810
They need people who look like them too.

00:31:35.810 --> 00:31:37.410
So we have to press a little bit

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.580
for some of these
professional associations,

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:42.730
go find some black and brown people

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:45.250
to bring into these teams
to help these young people

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.860
because they need people
they can relate to

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:49.560
without having to explain everything

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:50.760
'cause that gets tiring.

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:53.550
- Yeah, you're exactly right.

00:31:53.550 --> 00:31:54.560
You're both exactly right.

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:56.413
'Cause you can't just have that.

00:31:57.371 --> 00:31:58.550
You can't have all the onus be

00:31:58.550 --> 00:32:00.670
on the person who's coming to therapy.

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:02.740
Like, they don't need to have to bring,

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:04.330
they shouldn't be trying
to teach the provider

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:05.483
about their situation.

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:07.860
Especially if someone's struggling.

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:10.170
There's already too much going on there.

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:12.610
So, I mean, it's so
important in so many ways.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:14.380
And it cuts across everything too.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:15.840
So Dr. Alfiee, as you mentioned,

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:17.490
I mean, for me as a neuroscientist,

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:20.290
it cuts across that to how
are we asking those questions

00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:23.220
and are we taking all those
things into consideration,

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:25.500
or are we kind of fitting
people into one box

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:26.970
and saying, this is just the way it is.

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:28.540
And then even if we're looking

00:32:28.540 --> 00:32:30.350
at a specific mental health challenge,

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:32.220
are we not even taking into account

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:34.528
some of the other factors
that could contribute to that?

00:32:34.528 --> 00:32:35.600
So I think both of these,

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:36.920
all the points that you all raised

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.010
are so vital and important.

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:41.750
And a big piece of that too is just,

00:32:41.750 --> 00:32:43.530
I mean, people have said
this in other settings too,

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.430
but when we're trying to help
people get access to resources

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:49.640
or someone who will care
for them, it's a matter of

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:52.030
are those people actually
listening to your story

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:55.210
or are they just putting
you in a box and saying,

00:32:55.210 --> 00:32:57.260
this is what I think you are dealing with,

00:32:57.260 --> 00:32:58.970
without even hearing
where you're coming from.

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:00.860
And I mean, that ties into
the cultural competency too.

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:01.940
But that happens so often.

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:03.740
- No, that's right.

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:05.010
When you said it, the
first thing I thought

00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:07.250
is they see Doug on
campus, Appalachian State,

00:33:07.250 --> 00:33:08.692
we know where Appalachian State is.

00:33:08.692 --> 00:33:10.010
They're like, yeah, athletes, right?

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:12.370
And yeah, he is, but you
don't know me like that.

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:13.400
You don't know what I'm here for.

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:15.900
I could be here getting a
PhD in neuroscience, right,

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:17.600
like Dr. Addy, so yeah.

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:19.823
And how othering that is, right?

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:22.610
When you make those assumptions,

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:24.220
you make people feel like the other.

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:27.100
Because the idea is
everybody here looks like,

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:29.280
if I'm white, everybody
here looks like me.

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:31.060
So anybody who doesn't look like me,

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:33.940
oh, the only way I can
conceive of you being here

00:33:33.940 --> 00:33:36.780
is you have to be an athlete
on a scholarship, right?

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:41.080
And that's hurtful because
we know, internally,

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:43.330
as the person on the receiving end,

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:45.047
we know that's what people think, right?

00:33:45.047 --> 00:33:47.080
And we don't wanna live like that.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:49.140
So that stuff weighs on your psyche

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:50.747
in addition to looking around.

00:33:50.747 --> 00:33:53.200
And as we used to say, my
family's from Mississippi,

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:54.550
you the fly in the buttermilk.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:56.261
I don't wanna be the
fly in the buttermilk.

00:33:56.261 --> 00:33:57.094
That ain't comfortable.

00:33:57.094 --> 00:33:58.755
That's not fun.

00:33:58.755 --> 00:34:00.290
So when you said that, Dr. Addy,

00:34:00.290 --> 00:34:02.560
it just made me think
it's that othering piece

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:03.810
that I think can be really hard

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:06.610
and make it really difficult
and challenging for people.

00:34:08.090 --> 00:34:09.470
- I think one thing that's
great that you're both doing

00:34:09.470 --> 00:34:11.260
is you're building community too.

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:12.640
I mean, Doug, even when
you were talking about

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:13.473
the group therapy.

00:34:13.473 --> 00:34:14.660
Like, that's given people community.

00:34:14.660 --> 00:34:16.370
They're coming out of isolation.

00:34:16.370 --> 00:34:17.540
Like, oh, that person's dealing

00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:19.170
with the same thing that I'm dealing with.

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:21.747
But if nobody voices it,
then no one hears that.

00:34:21.747 --> 00:34:23.550
And you're all going back
to the neuroscience too.

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:24.530
I mean, there's so much evidence

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:27.270
about how community
actually shapes our brains

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:29.570
and makes it easier for us to deal

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:31.050
with some of these
mental health challenges.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:33.773
So, I mean, you're both
tapping into that already.

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:35.990
- I love it.

00:34:35.990 --> 00:34:37.972
- You got the science to
support it too, I take it.

00:34:37.972 --> 00:34:40.296
- Okay, okay, I feel smart today.

00:34:40.296 --> 00:34:41.508
Thank you, thank you.

00:34:41.508 --> 00:34:42.341
I take it.
- Yeah, bring it on.

00:34:42.341 --> 00:34:43.419
I'm learning from both of you too.

00:34:43.419 --> 00:34:45.070
(laughs)

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:46.610
On that note, I was
gonna have you both share

00:34:46.610 --> 00:34:48.610
a little bit too about
what have you learned

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:49.910
from the young people
that you've worked with?

00:34:49.910 --> 00:34:51.210
'Cause I know this is a two-way street.

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:52.820
I mean, you're bringing
obviously a lot of knowledge

00:34:52.820 --> 00:34:53.670
and information to them,

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:56.690
but I would imagine that there's
things that you take away

00:34:56.690 --> 00:34:58.090
that they teach you as well.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:34:59.843
- Yeah.

00:34:59.843 --> 00:35:01.510
So for me, I think it's kind of easy.

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:02.343
A couple of things.

00:35:02.343 --> 00:35:06.700
One is young people are
more eager to engage

00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:09.380
and invest in this space
than we give them credit for.

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:11.440
Two, they have loads of ideas

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:14.910
if we just listen to
them without judgment.

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:15.810
Just listen to 'em, right?

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:17.410
Not every idea is gonna be perfect,

00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:19.370
but not every idea we present as adults

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:20.763
is perfect for them either.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.960
Three, I feel like
they're eager to partner

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:26.290
if they're asked in the right way.

00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:28.440
So we kinda talked about that
a little bit when we started.

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:32.100
If you ask nicely and if you ask

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:35.150
and then be quiet and listen,
they will talk to you.

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:37.050
I think too often what young people,

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:39.100
what we've learned from
our research is that

00:35:39.100 --> 00:35:41.340
we as adults will ask.

00:35:41.340 --> 00:35:43.210
But then as soon as we finished asking,

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:44.340
we're answering for them.

00:35:44.340 --> 00:35:46.640
We don't give them an opportunity
to express themselves.

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:49.910
So it's this idea of, I
call it active listening.

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:51.780
So first, I have to close my mouth, right,

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:54.027
and I love to talk, so I have to work on,

00:35:54.027 --> 00:35:56.710
I just kinda sit on my
hands, close my mouth

00:35:56.710 --> 00:35:58.650
after I ask the question and listen.

00:35:58.650 --> 00:36:02.470
And you're listening not as
you're crafting an answer,

00:36:02.470 --> 00:36:04.570
you're listening to take in

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:06.610
and receive what the
young people are saying.

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:08.570
So I'm learning how to listen from them.

00:36:08.570 --> 00:36:09.990
And then the final thing I would say

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:14.613
is I'm learning how to
be more unapologetic.

00:36:15.700 --> 00:36:17.830
Like, if nothing, I'm telling you,

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:20.030
the younger millennials and Gen Z

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:22.870
ain't apologizing for being black

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:24.930
or being brown or being
queer or whatever it is.

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:27.370
And I have really truly learned

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.110
to stop apologizing for who I am

00:36:29.110 --> 00:36:31.680
and just to like own it and inherit it

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:34.115
and inhabit it and stand in it.

00:36:34.115 --> 00:36:36.165
And so that's been really powerful for me

00:36:37.322 --> 00:36:41.360
to keep going, right, because
there's so many ways in which,

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:43.510
I'm going back to my
experiences as an academic,

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:45.900
people were like, yeah, but yeah,

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:47.470
we already know how to treat depression,

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:49.210
we already know how to treat anxiety.

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.030
And I'm thinking, but if you did,

00:36:51.030 --> 00:36:52.600
we wouldn't have these health disparities,

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:54.980
we wouldn't have these
differences in who accesses care,

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:56.970
we wouldn't have greater attrition

00:36:56.970 --> 00:36:59.250
or drop-off rates when people are black

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:00.810
if you really knew what you were doing.

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:02.950
So clearly, you don't have the answers.

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:05.090
So what I'm learning is to not apologize

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:08.190
for what it is that I wanna study

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:10.180
because these young people don't
apologize for who they are.

00:37:10.180 --> 00:37:12.020
So they teach me in those ways too.

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:14.790
So that's not exactly the
answer to the question,

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:16.959
but that is what I'm learning
from the young people.

00:37:16.959 --> 00:37:19.321
- Yeah, yeah.
- That's it.

00:37:19.321 --> 00:37:20.270
- By golly, that's a great answer.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:22.440
Because like that's
actually the same thing

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:23.963
that I'm learning from them.

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:27.263
This generation is so resilient.

00:37:28.913 --> 00:37:32.070
I mean, I keep going back to
this George Floyd situation.

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:34.530
But if you look at all the protests

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:36.130
and everything that was going on

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:39.000
and like all this was kind
of led by young people.

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:40.900
Those are the ones that they're
learning how to strategize

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:44.210
and mobilize and they know how to,

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:47.540
like even, a lot of the
African American students

00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:49.230
on our campus at App State,

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:51.810
they had a whole list of
demands that they wanted.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:55.270
I mean, they don't take no for an answer.

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:57.540
And I think that's kinda
what I'm learning for 'em,

00:37:57.540 --> 00:38:01.810
is like how hard and how
passionate they will work

00:38:01.810 --> 00:38:04.270
to get what they feel is right

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:08.170
and how they're not gonna accept the world

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:09.500
being the same way that it is.

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:13.350
So, yeah, that's what
I'm learning from them.

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:15.310
- Yeah, I agree with both of you.

00:38:15.310 --> 00:38:17.620
I mean, in some ways it's humbling too.

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:19.470
I mean, even looking at this podcast.

00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:22.840
10 plus years into my career.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:24.500
I'm seeing those that are coming up

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:26.320
doing stuff like this from the get-go

00:38:26.320 --> 00:38:27.850
even while they're still training.

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:28.820
I can imagine.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:30.390
So just the level of effort.

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:32.530
And like you said, just
knowing who they are

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:34.090
and saying we're not gonna
take no for an answer

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:37.020
and really just jumping in
and making an impact early on.

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:38.620
It's inspiring in a lot of ways.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:42.810
So to pivot just a little bit

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:44.547
'cause I know we wanted
to still touch on it

00:38:44.547 --> 00:38:45.740
'cause both of you have done aspects

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:47.670
around suicide prevention as well.

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:50.480
I mean, obviously it's not light
topic, it's not easy topic,

00:38:50.480 --> 00:38:51.620
but it's again, the same thing

00:38:51.620 --> 00:38:54.470
where I feel like there's so
much stigma associated with it

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:56.660
that oftentimes in so many circles

00:38:56.660 --> 00:38:59.020
it just doesn't get talked
about and it just gets ignored

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:01.240
so people are kinda left to their own,

00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:03.990
whatever concept they have about it.

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:06.083
So I just wanted to ask both of you

00:39:06.083 --> 00:39:08.820
like what you've seen in
terms of trying to move

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:11.690
the needle on that and
really breaking that stigma

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:13.130
so that people can get to a place

00:39:13.130 --> 00:39:14.030
of really talking about it.

00:39:14.030 --> 00:39:16.180
And I guess we can start
just misconceptions

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:17.360
that you've both noticed in your work

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:20.323
that people have around
suicide and suicide prevention.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:24.063
- I guess one thing
that I could talk about,

00:39:24.063 --> 00:39:25.410
just a quick little shout out.

00:39:25.410 --> 00:39:28.690
So one of my colleagues
that's in this space,

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:29.970
her name is Fonda Bryant,

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:32.920
but she uses this
question, persuade, refer,

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:34.640
the QPR training.

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:36.270
That's a huge thing that she loves to do.

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:40.320
And it's made it a really
easy way to like kinda

00:39:41.410 --> 00:39:44.020
give people a few steps
that way they can help

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:45.583
learn how to prevent suicide.

00:39:46.492 --> 00:39:51.492
That's one way that some of
the education has taken place.

00:39:51.550 --> 00:39:54.120
And I guess the misconceptions is that

00:39:55.770 --> 00:40:00.770
I know a lot of people would
think that when they commit,

00:40:01.390 --> 00:40:03.920
when they suffer a death by suicide

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:05.630
that the pain is gonna end,

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:07.310
like that is all gonna go away

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:08.900
and then they'll just
be able to start over.

00:40:08.900 --> 00:40:11.393
And that's not really the case.

00:40:14.296 --> 00:40:17.710
They have a short-term problem,

00:40:17.710 --> 00:40:20.100
but they're trying to fix it
with a long-term solution.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:22.603
And I think that's the
misconception around it.

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:26.480
I mean, that's kinda all I
really had to say about it

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:30.062
because I still struggle
with trying to find

00:40:30.062 --> 00:40:33.080
some of the things that
I've could have done better

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:34.150
with my best friend's life

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:36.920
and some of the ways I
could've provided him

00:40:36.920 --> 00:40:40.433
with more steps or more
access to resources

00:40:40.433 --> 00:40:43.423
that that way suicide
wasn't really an option.

00:40:44.390 --> 00:40:45.810
- Yeah, and I know
that's not an easy topic

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:47.850
so definitely wanna just acknowledge that

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:49.660
and appreciate you being willing to go,

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:50.600
go to that place as well.

00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:52.570
'Cause I think that empowers
a lot of people too,

00:40:52.570 --> 00:40:55.410
just as we continue to break
through those misconceptions.

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:57.250
And I know that your foundations,

00:40:57.250 --> 00:41:00.450
like Legacy Foundation in a
lot of ways for AJ's life.

00:41:00.450 --> 00:41:01.990
So definitely appreciate

00:41:01.990 --> 00:41:03.954
the way that you're continuing to do that.

00:41:03.954 --> 00:41:05.350
How about you, Dr. Alfiee?

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:07.550
- Yeah, so first, I wanna just quickly,

00:41:07.550 --> 00:41:10.197
I meant to do at the front
end a whole space for Doug

00:41:10.197 --> 00:41:12.960
and the loss of your
friend, 'cause that's a lot.

00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:14.113
It's a lot to carry.

00:41:15.511 --> 00:41:20.511
And like Dr. Addy, appreciate
you for being vocal and open

00:41:20.651 --> 00:41:23.550
and taking action to try to
do something in his honor.

00:41:23.550 --> 00:41:26.120
And the other thing I'll
say, now this is the auntie,

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:28.300
I'm sorry, this is the auntie in me.

00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:29.450
So it's gonna come out.

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:33.920
But I think it's important
for the survivors,

00:41:33.920 --> 00:41:37.330
the ones who are still here, to understand

00:41:37.330 --> 00:41:42.330
that when a person is
determined to complete a suicide

00:41:42.350 --> 00:41:44.270
it's difficult for us to stop them.

00:41:44.270 --> 00:41:48.040
But I think the key is when
they're determined, right,

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:50.890
not everybody, not even most people,

00:41:50.890 --> 00:41:53.350
are determined to complete it.

00:41:53.350 --> 00:41:56.620
I think what I've learned in
terms of suicide prevention

00:41:56.620 --> 00:41:58.750
is that people wanna stop the pain.

00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:00.510
They don't really wanna die, right?

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:02.550
I do not believe that anybody,

00:42:02.550 --> 00:42:05.350
almost anybody who dies by
suicide that the goal is to die.

00:42:05.350 --> 00:42:07.430
The goal is to stop the pain.

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:10.670
And just like you said, Doug,
it's a permanent solution

00:42:10.670 --> 00:42:13.280
to what often is a temporary problem.

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:15.930
But sometimes a temporary
goes on too long for people

00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:18.400
and they just don't see any other way out.

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:20.280
So what I've learned
about suicide prevention

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:23.340
is that being silent about it,

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:28.340
about these are people's
experiences, that is not helpful

00:42:28.540 --> 00:42:31.280
especially for those of us
from underserved communities

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:33.943
or people of color or
black folks in particular.

00:42:35.020 --> 00:42:39.860
I've also learned that
it's not a bad thing

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:40.710
to talk about it.

00:42:40.710 --> 00:42:43.200
It's not a bad thing to ask the question.

00:42:43.200 --> 00:42:46.270
You do not plant the
idea in somebody's head

00:42:46.270 --> 00:42:48.710
when you ask them if
they're feeling suicidal,

00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:50.600
are they having suicidal thoughts?

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:51.890
Those are all things that I've learned.

00:42:51.890 --> 00:42:55.760
I've learned that it's
important for us as advocates,

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:58.270
as professionals, as loved ones

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:02.543
to remember that we can do
everything in our power,

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.650
but we cannot fix people.

00:43:05.650 --> 00:43:06.483
I tell people that.

00:43:06.483 --> 00:43:07.760
You can't fix other people.

00:43:07.760 --> 00:43:11.130
The only thing we can do is
be present, be available,

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:13.570
always let them know that
we're there for them,

00:43:13.570 --> 00:43:16.580
try to find resources and
tools to share with them,

00:43:16.580 --> 00:43:19.660
educate ourselves so that
we can become more aware.

00:43:19.660 --> 00:43:21.560
And I think once we've done all of that,

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:23.543
we have to visit and this
is another auntie part,

00:43:23.543 --> 00:43:25.600
we have to forgive ourselves.

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:28.240
I have a really close girlfriend
who lost her son to suicide

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:32.163
about, maybe 25 years ago.

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:35.980
Yeah, 24, 25 years ago.

00:43:35.980 --> 00:43:38.450
And ever since then she and
I have been close partners.

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:41.200
We go around and do a lot of
advocating internationally.

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:43.890
We've been to a couple
of different places.

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:47.400
And she shares her story
about losing her son

00:43:48.270 --> 00:43:50.460
after losing her first
husband in a car accident.

00:43:50.460 --> 00:43:53.790
So she believes that her son's suicide

00:43:53.790 --> 00:43:55.470
was related to complicated grief

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:57.040
that he never was able to heal.

00:43:57.040 --> 00:43:59.300
So he was six when he lost his dad

00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:01.043
and he died by suicide at 15.

00:44:02.360 --> 00:44:04.780
And so those are just some
of the different things

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:06.260
that I've learned about
suicide prevention.

00:44:06.260 --> 00:44:07.940
But I think the most important thing is,

00:44:07.940 --> 00:44:09.330
one of the most important
things that I've learned

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:13.870
is we still don't know enough
about suicide prevention

00:44:13.870 --> 00:44:15.360
in black communities specifically

00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:17.290
because we don't have
enough research out there.

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:18.610
Now, we know anecdotally,

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:22.080
we know how to have these
conversations in our community.

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:24.460
I want nonprofits and funders, right,

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:26.850
that's part of why we're
so invested in research,

00:44:26.850 --> 00:44:30.110
to go out there and do the
work to give us more ideas

00:44:30.110 --> 00:44:31.700
about how do we take care of ourselves.

00:44:31.700 --> 00:44:34.400
And part of that is
learning how to understand

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:39.160
the relationship between
racism, discrimination,

00:44:39.160 --> 00:44:43.320
all the isms, right, of the
phobias that people experience

00:44:43.320 --> 00:44:44.200
and suicide.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:46.270
When you have the weight
of all those things

00:44:46.270 --> 00:44:49.573
compounding your depression,
your anxiety, or your trauma,

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:53.580
I think it can be a
really awful perfect storm

00:44:53.580 --> 00:44:54.413
that contributes to it.

00:44:54.413 --> 00:44:56.070
But we just have not spent enough time

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:59.930
and invested in this country
in getting that knowledge

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:01.870
to be able to help people on the scale

00:45:01.870 --> 00:45:02.703
that we would like to.

00:45:02.703 --> 00:45:05.480
So that's why with Doug,
what you do is so critical,

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:08.080
Dr. Addy having this
podcast is so critical.

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:08.930
I know what I do,

00:45:08.930 --> 00:45:11.050
again, I'm being unapologetic
like the young folk,

00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:14.420
what I do is so critical
because we are the ones

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:16.410
who are gonna put a spotlight on this

00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:17.670
and show people, right?

00:45:17.670 --> 00:45:18.880
So I hope if everybody listens,

00:45:18.880 --> 00:45:20.100
that people listen to this, Doug,

00:45:20.100 --> 00:45:21.770
what they hear you say is

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:23.690
y'all need to go get some therapists

00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:26.890
and bring 'em, like App State,
all these other white school,

00:45:26.890 --> 00:45:29.280
bring them therapists to them kids

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:30.740
in the safe place for them.

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:33.150
Stop asking them to go over to CAPS,

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:34.480
Counseling and Psychological Services.

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:37.590
So things like that,
that's what we have to do

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:41.293
to support our young people
and to help prevent suicide.

00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:44.050
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:45:44.050 --> 00:45:46.640
I mean, so many important aspects

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:47.473
that you've both brought out.

00:45:47.473 --> 00:45:49.510
And not easy, like I've
said several times,

00:45:49.510 --> 00:45:51.210
but I mean, it's critical.

00:45:51.210 --> 00:45:52.557
'Cause like I said, if
we don't talk about it,

00:45:52.557 --> 00:45:55.090
that's where things
sit, where they've been,

00:45:55.090 --> 00:45:55.923
I'm telling you, to both
of what you're doing

00:45:55.923 --> 00:45:57.510
to really move the conversation forward,

00:45:57.510 --> 00:45:59.380
move the resources forward.

00:45:59.380 --> 00:46:01.340
Just make sure that people know.

00:46:01.340 --> 00:46:03.757
So, I mean, it goes back to
that community in isolation too.

00:46:03.757 --> 00:46:05.423
And just making sure, Dr.
Alfiee, like you said,

00:46:05.423 --> 00:46:06.580
that we're present

00:46:06.580 --> 00:46:08.880
and we're willing to
have those conversations.

00:46:09.750 --> 00:46:11.410
Other question I usually
like to, oh, go ahead.

00:46:11.410 --> 00:46:13.150
I don't know if there's
something else you wanna,

00:46:13.150 --> 00:46:14.430
you good, okay.

00:46:14.430 --> 00:46:15.740
Also, just like to ask folks

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:17.440
in the midst of all these challenges too,

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:20.520
what gives you hope especially
in the work that you're doing

00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:21.353
with the young people?

00:46:21.353 --> 00:46:22.900
You've hinted at some of that already,

00:46:22.900 --> 00:46:24.033
but we're curious if both of you

00:46:24.033 --> 00:46:26.280
kind of elaborate on that piece too.

00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:27.430
- That's easy for me.

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:29.180
This right here gives me hope,

00:46:29.180 --> 00:46:31.320
knowing that there are
people younger than me

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:34.000
who care about this stuff, who are black,

00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:36.970
black, black, blackity black,
who care about this stuff.

00:46:36.970 --> 00:46:38.080
That gives me hope.

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:39.980
Because we know that we're not alone.

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:42.150
And there can never be too
many of us doing this work.

00:46:42.150 --> 00:46:44.210
So every new foundation
I see, more power to you

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:45.830
because that's more of us to do the work

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:46.923
to reach more people.

00:46:48.020 --> 00:46:51.080
Me having being able
to start the foundation

00:46:51.965 --> 00:46:54.330
and to fund raise the way
that we were able to do

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:57.347
in the first year, that gives me hope.

00:46:57.347 --> 00:46:58.900
And a lot of our funding comes from people

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:00.260
who are not black.

00:47:00.260 --> 00:47:02.070
And so it gives me hope

00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:04.110
that other people are paying attention

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:07.340
and care and wanna help
our young people of color.

00:47:07.340 --> 00:47:08.173
Those two things.

00:47:08.173 --> 00:47:09.630
And then finally, what gives me hope

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:11.750
is just young people in general.

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:14.100
Like, just knowing how, again,

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:16.420
they just inhabit their own space

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:17.640
and they know who they are.

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:21.110
And like you said, Dr. Addy,
jumping out of undergrad

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:23.850
or coming right out of,
or being in residency

00:47:23.850 --> 00:47:25.850
or graduate school and they're
gonna start their podcast,

00:47:25.850 --> 00:47:27.330
they gonna start their own foundation,

00:47:27.330 --> 00:47:29.180
that gives me hope because it lets me know

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:33.130
that they are not broken,
especially our kids of color,

00:47:33.130 --> 00:47:37.253
by the systems that impact
and weigh on them so heavily.

00:47:39.004 --> 00:47:40.310
But the most important thing today

00:47:40.310 --> 00:47:42.780
that's given me hope is being
able to be here with you too.

00:47:42.780 --> 00:47:44.670
That just gives me all
the hope in the world.

00:47:44.670 --> 00:47:46.320
And so more power to both of you.

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:49.190
- Wow, that's mutual.

00:47:49.190 --> 00:47:50.190
I can tell you that.

00:47:51.540 --> 00:47:53.070
- Yeah, I would say the same points.

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:54.350
The things that give me hope

00:47:54.350 --> 00:47:59.330
is just really seeing the
progress with the youth.

00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:02.170
You come in, you spend time with them

00:48:02.170 --> 00:48:05.360
and then a year later you see
that they're in a better space

00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:07.790
than they were when you first met them.

00:48:07.790 --> 00:48:09.620
And now they're ready to put some action

00:48:09.620 --> 00:48:12.600
behind some of the feelings
and experiences that they have.

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:16.010
And Dr. Alfiee, to one of
the points that you said,

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:20.870
just how many people
gravitate towards a cause

00:48:20.870 --> 00:48:24.390
or experience when you may not even know

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:27.160
or they might not even be black or they,

00:48:27.160 --> 00:48:28.590
you don't even know why they do it,

00:48:28.590 --> 00:48:31.790
but somewhere down the
line they may had a friend

00:48:31.790 --> 00:48:33.620
or experience that
they're really trust him

00:48:33.620 --> 00:48:37.130
and that's why they
wanna join on the cause.

00:48:37.130 --> 00:48:41.930
So just seeing people
share that passion with me

00:48:41.930 --> 00:48:44.462
is something that really
helps and gives me hope.

00:48:44.462 --> 00:48:49.462
And just seeing that things
are changing day by day,

00:48:51.870 --> 00:48:54.520
you just gotta continue to
take it one day at a time.

00:48:56.550 --> 00:48:57.383
- That's so good.

00:48:57.383 --> 00:48:58.850
Well, it's gonna sound like a cliche,

00:48:58.850 --> 00:49:01.600
but I know that both of you
give a lot of people hope,

00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:03.150
just in the work that you're doing.

00:49:03.150 --> 00:49:04.530
And to be completely honest,

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:06.700
I mean, we talked about this
before we jumped on too,

00:49:06.700 --> 00:49:08.750
but how quickly you both were willing

00:49:08.750 --> 00:49:09.990
to jump on this podcast too.

00:49:09.990 --> 00:49:11.950
I mean, personally, that
gives me a lot of hope,

00:49:11.950 --> 00:49:13.610
seeing what you all are doing.

00:49:13.610 --> 00:49:14.850
And I feel like in some ways,

00:49:14.850 --> 00:49:16.020
and I'm in the place of privilege,

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:17.730
to be able to bring all
these different folks

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:20.480
on to this podcast to kinda amplify

00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:21.590
what you all are already doing.

00:49:21.590 --> 00:49:22.950
'Cause I mean, you're
already doing the work.

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:24.860
This is just another platform.

00:49:24.860 --> 00:49:26.180
It's kinda bringing those pieces together,

00:49:26.180 --> 00:49:27.140
and I think to talk about it

00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:28.620
in a way that doesn't always get as much,

00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:30.070
quote, unquote airplay.

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:32.820
So yeah, I'm deeply
appreciative to both of you.

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:33.653
- Thank you.

00:49:33.653 --> 00:49:34.530
- And I think this is gonna go a long way.

00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:36.640
I hope our listeners will feel that too

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:37.740
when they listen to this episode.

00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:41.173
So any closing pearls of
wisdom from either of you?

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:44.650
You dropped a lot of those already.

00:49:44.650 --> 00:49:45.483
- Yeah.

00:49:45.483 --> 00:49:47.010
(laughs)

00:49:47.010 --> 00:49:50.290
- The only thing I would
add is curate your news

00:49:50.290 --> 00:49:52.780
